  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes crystal-field splitting and symmetry relationships for a samarium ion in a boron cage–type environment. In the upper left, a schematic energy-level diagram shows a multiplet labeled \\(X_7\\) separating into lower and upper branches, with only weakly split states remaining near the center. An inset depicts the local crystal structure around Sm inside a cubic framework, suggesting the origin of this splitting.\n\nThe upper right connects a three-dimensional polyhedron to several projected planar motifs. Colored dashed lines map atomic positions from the polyhedral coordination environment onto square or rectangular planes, highlighting how the same local geometry can be viewed along different symmetry directions.\n\nThe lower row focuses on these projected symmetries and the associated orbital or multipolar patterns. Square, rectangular, and hexagonal outlines contain colored points and curved arrows that indicate rotational or circulating components around symmetry-related sites. Dashed axes and central markers emphasize mirror lines, diagonals, and a center labeled \\(\\Gamma\\). Labels such as \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), \\(M\\), \\(K\\), and \\(\\bar{\\Gamma}\\) mark high-symmetry points in reciprocal-space-like diagrams. Overall, the figure appears to relate the local crystal environment to symmetry-adapted electronic states and their characteristic directional textures.",
